# YouTube to Note — environment variables for API keys (keeps secrets off disk)
#
# The plugin reads these from the actual process environment at Obsidian launch.
# It does NOT read a .env file. Set these in your shell / system environment
# BEFORE starting Obsidian, then enable "Use Environment Variables" in the
# plugin settings and leave the in-app key fields empty.
#
# Example — prefix the Obsidian launch with the vars (macOS/Linux):
#   YTC_GEMINI_API_KEY=... open -a Obsidian        # macOS
#   YTC_GEMINI_API_KEY=... obsidian &               # Linux (if on PATH)
# Or add `export YTC_GEMINI_API_KEY=...` lines to ~/.zshrc / ~/.bashrc so they
# persist across launches.
#
# The prefix defaults to YTC and is a settings field — "Environment variable
# prefix", directly under the "Env Variables" toggle. Change it there (e.g. to
# YT2N) and rename these variables to match. Before that field existed the
# prefix could only be set by hand-editing data.json; it no longer can.
#
# Only the keys you actually use are required, and ANY ONE provider is enough —
# Gemini, Groq, OpenRouter, Hugging Face or Ollama Cloud. No provider is a
# prerequisite for another; local Ollama needs no key at all.

YTC_GEMINI_API_KEY=
YTC_GROQ_API_KEY=
YTC_OLLAMA_API_KEY=
YTC_HUGGINGFACE_API_KEY=
YTC_OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
